Major releases should align with milestone completion.

Recommended mapping:

| Release | Milestone |
|----------|-----------|
| v0.1 | M1 |
| v0.2 | M2 |
| v0.3 | M3 |
| v0.4 | M4 |
| v0.5 | M5 |
| v0.6 | M6 |
| v0.7 | M7 |
| v0.8 | M8 |
| v0.9 | M9 |
| v1.0 | M10 |

Intermediate releases may be used for internal validation.

---

# 19. Success Metrics

Implementation success should be measured using:

Delivery

- milestone completion;
- lead time;
- cycle time.

Quality

- review pass rate;
- QA pass rate;
- defect escape rate.

Operations

- deployment success rate;
- rollback frequency;
- production incidents.

Business

- onboarding completion;
- transaction success rate;
- customer engagement.

---

# 20. Risks

Primary implementation risks include:

- architectural drift;
- documentation drift;
- ownership violations;
- uncontrolled scope expansion;
- insufficient automated testing;
- delayed dependency delivery.

Each risk should have:

- owner;
- mitigation plan;
- review cadence.

---

# 21. Exit Criteria for v1.0

Version 1.0 is considered complete when:

- all Blueprint capabilities are implemented;
- all mandatory Engineering standards are satisfied;
- UAT is approved;
- Production Readiness is achieved;
- Release Strategy has been successfully executed;
- documentation is synchronized with implementation.

---

# 22. Future Evolution

Future releases should build upon v1.0 using:

- Product Decisions
- ADRs
- Product Backlog
- Module Implementation Packages
- Project Task Catalog

Major architectural redesigns should be avoided unless justified by new business requirements.

---

# 23. Roadmap Governance

The Product Owner owns roadmap priorities.

The Solution Architect owns technical feasibility.

The Project Manager owns execution planning.

The Release Manager owns production delivery.

Roadmap changes require approval through the established governance process.

---

# 24. Definition of Roadmap Ready

The v1 Implementation Roadmap is considered ready when:

- implementation milestones are defined;
- business capabilities are mapped;
- dependencies are identified;
- parallel workstreams are defined;
- quality milestones are defined;
- release alignment is documented;
- risks are identified;
- governance responsibilities are defined;
- success criteria are established.

This document becomes the authoritative strategic implementation roadmap following Blueprint Freeze v1.0.